the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind $(\\log_2 x)^2$ if $\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"36e32188570689c661ab6751eca93ee42c81211565847ec1c09456f6fed76ad5"},"seconds":56.77591633796692,"seed":726330843233412354,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the equation \\(\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\), we will start by expressing all logarithms with the same base, which will simplify our calculations.\n\n1. **Express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of \\(\\log_2 x\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute \\(\\log_8 x\\) into the original equation:**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) in terms of \\(\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{\\log_2 8}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) back into the equation:**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify \\(\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right)\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 (\\log_2 x) - \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   So the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 (\\log_2 x) - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Let \\(y = \\log_2 (\\log_2 x)\\). Substitute \\(y\\) into the equation:**\n   \\[\n   y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{y}{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Solve for \\(y\\):**\n   \\[\n   y - \\frac{y}{3} = \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3y - y}{3} = \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2y}{3} = \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2y = 3 \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Recall that \\(y = \\log_2 (\\log_2 x)\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\):**\n   Let \\(z = \\log_2 x\\). Then:\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 z = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = 2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{\\frac{3}{2}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = 3^{\\frac{3}{2}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   (\\log_2 x)^2 = (3^{\\frac{3}{2}})^2 = 3^3 = 27\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{27}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the equation \\(\\log_2 (\\log_8 x) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\), we will start by expressing all logarithms with the same base, which will simplify our calculations.\n\n1. **Express \\(\\log_8 x\\) in terms of \\(\\log_2 x\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{\\log_2 8}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 x = \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute \\(\\log_8 x\\) into the original equation:**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) in terms of \\(\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{\\log_2 8}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\log_2 8 = 3\\), we have:\n   \\[\n   \\log_8 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\(\\log_8 (\\log_2 x)\\) back into the equation:**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify \\(\\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right)\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 \\left( \\frac{\\log_2 x}{3} \\right) = \\log_2 (\\log_2 x) - \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   So the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 (\\log_2 x) - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{\\log_2 (\\log_2 x)}{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Let \\(y = \\log_2 (\\log_2 x)\\). Substitute \\(y\\) into the equation:**\n   \\[\n   y - \\log_2 3 = \\frac{y}{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Solve for \\(y\\):**\n   \\[\n   y - \\frac{y}{3} = \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3y - y}{3} = \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2y}{3} = \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2y = 3 \\log_2 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   y = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n8. **Recall that \\(y = \\log_2 (\\log_2 x)\\):**\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 (\\log_2 x) = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Find \\((\\log_2 x)^2\\):**\n   Let \\(z = \\log_2 x\\). Then:\n   \\[\n   \\log_2 z = \\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = 2^{\\frac{3 \\log_2 3}{2}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = (2^{\\log_2 3})^{\\frac{3}{2}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   z = 3^{\\frac{3}{2}}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   (\\log_2 x)^2 = (3^{\\frac{3}{2}})^2 = 3^3 = 27\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}\n\\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:434"}
